    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Drug-induced hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, recovering (E23.1)</content>. LH 2.4 and FSH 3.1 mIU/mL are back in range and testosterone has risen from 112 to 238 ng/dL, still low. Estradiol and prolactin are normal. Hemoglobin has normalized. [S]<list><item>Continue watchful recovery without testosterone therapy.</item><item>Testosterone, LH, FSH, and hemoglobin in eight weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bilateral gynecomastia, improving (N62)</content>. Smaller and less tender now that estradiol is normal. [S]<list><item>Continue watchful waiting.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, with partial response at eight weeks (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 12, down 20 percent from 15 after eight weeks of psychotherapy alone: a partial response. He chose to add an antidepressant. Bupropion suits his wish to avoid sexual side effects and weight change; he has no seizure risk factors. No past mania or hypomania. Suicide risk remains low. [S]<list><item>Start bupropion XL 150 mg each morning; nurse telephone check in two weeks for sleep, anxiety, blood pressure, and safety.</item><item>Continue weekly psychotherapy.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Breast examination for size, tenderness, and any discrete mass</td><td>Every visit until the gynecomastia settles</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Glandular tissue about 3 cm behind each nipple, softer, mildly tender; no discrete mass</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Testosterone, estradiol, LH, and FSH</td><td>Every 6 to 8 weeks after the exposure stops, until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Testosterone 238 ng/dL (low, rising); estradiol 26 pg/mL; LH 2.4 and FSH 3.1 mIU/mL, now in range</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Prolactin after the estrogen clears</td><td>Repeat once after a raised value</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12.4 ng/mL, normal, down from 21.6; the rise was estrogen-related</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin while testosterone is low</td><td>With each hormone check until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>13.6 g/dL, back in range</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Testosterone therapy</td><td>Only for persistent, symptomatic, confirmed testosterone deficiency</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not given while the axis recovers: it would suppress LH and FSH again and is not indicated for a transient, drug-induced low testosterone</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12, down 20 percent from 15: partial response; bupropion added. Six weeks since the last score, two weeks beyond the monthly interval, because this review was timed to the hormone tests; no harm, as he had weekly therapy sessions and no suicidal thoughts</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>With the PHQ-9 when anxiety symptoms were present at baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6, down from 8</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior since the last visit; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Protective factors unchanged; safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Bipolar screen before starting an antidepressant</td><td>At the initial workup; repeat if hypomanic features emerge</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Still no history of elevated mood, reduced need for sleep, or racing thoughts</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antidepressant tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Due</td><td>Nurse telephone check booked for two weeks from today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy, sleep, running, and social contact</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven sessions; running about 25 km a week; early waking</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant, or antipsychotic monitoring (levels, metabolic tests, AIMS)</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on any of these drugs</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Testosterone, LH, and FSH back in the reference range within six months of the last estrogen dose, without testosterone therapy. In progress; target 2026-08-04 [S]</item><item>Breast tenderness gone and gynecomastia reduced; a surgical opinion offered if it persists and troubles him. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by sixteen weeks, then remission (PHQ-9 below 5). In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Back to running about 40 km a week and changing at his club again. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EAA clinical practice guidelines: gynecomastia evaluation and management (2019); Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2018); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in two weeks; review in eight weeks with hormone tests and PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-04: sixteen-week review of hormone recovery and depression treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

